      "verified": true,
      "fine_trace_step": 1,
      "description": "The first `\\u` in the witness is accepted by the string-escape handler and initializes unicode-escape decode state. This is the first project statement on the vulnerable path that turns attacker-controlled bytes into the parser state later used by the stale-surrogate cleanup."
    },
    {
      "invariant_id": "r_unicode_escape_requires_four_digits_before_pending_resolution",
      "role": "root_cause",
      "file": "json_tokener.c",
      "function": "json_tokener_parse_ex",
      "line": 796,
      "operands": [
        "4",
        "tok->st_pos"
      ],
      "relation": {
        "op": "eq",
        "left": "4",
        "right": "tok->st_pos"
      },
      "verified": true,
      "verified_by": "a_unicode_escape_requires_four_digits_before_pending_resolution",
      "fine_trace_step": 7,
      "description": "Incomplete-escape EOF handling is wrong. After the second `\\u` has only one hex digit, this cleanup block runs solely on EOF, not on completion of four hex digits. That reuses stale `tok->got_hi_surrogate` from the earlier standalone high surrogate instead of returning with the partial escape state preserved. The fixed commit adds that missing early-return behavior for `tok->st_pos < 4` and never reaches this cleanup compare on an incomplete `\\uNNNN`."
    },
    {
      "invariant_id": "e_incomplete_escape_reaches_pending_resolution_gate",
      "role": "sink",
      "file": "json_tokener.c",
      "function": "json_tokener_parse_ex",
      "line": 800,
      "operands": [
        "tok->st_pos"
      ],
      "relation": {
        "op": "eq",
        "left": "tok->st_pos",
        "right": "tok->st_pos"
      },
      "verified": true,
      "verified_by": "a_incomplete_escape_reaches_pending_resolution_gate",
      "fine_trace_step": 8,
      "description": "ASan crash site. During EOF cleanup for the second, incomplete `\\ud`, the parser compares the accumulated print buffer against `utf8_replacement_char` with `strcmp`. Because `utf8_replacement_char` is a 3-byte global rather than a NUL-terminated C string, the compare reads past its end."
    },
